## p2-bf | Magnetic fields and forces | physics | 1 | phys2 | p2-rc
explain 2 | The Lorentz force
- $ F = q(E + v × B)
- The magnetic part is always perpendicular to the velocity, so it does no work.
- *Magnetic fields bend paths but never change speed.*
? Why does a magnetic field do no work on a moving charge? :: The force qv×B is always perpendicular to the velocity
> So charges in a uniform B field go in circles

quiz 3 | Radius of a charged particle's circle in a magnetic field?
* r = mv/(qB)
o r = qB/(mv)
o r = mvqB
o r = v/B
= qvB = mv²/r.
= Heavier ions curve less: that's how a mass spectrometer sorts them.
> The cyclotron frequency doesn't depend on speed

explain 4 | The cyclotron trick
- Period of the circle: 2πm/(qB). Independent of speed.
- So a fixed-frequency alternating voltage can kick particles every half turn as they spiral outward.
- *Ernest Lawrence's first cyclotron (1931) fitted in his hand.*
> Earth's field traps particles too

real 4 | Auroras are particles spiralling along Earth's field lines
- Charged particles from the Sun spiral around magnetic field lines toward the poles.
- They hit oxygen and nitrogen high in the atmosphere, which glow green and red.
- *The Van Allen belts are the trapped particles that bounce between the poles.*
> Currents feel magnetic forces too

quiz 3 | Force on a 2 m wire carrying 3 A at right angles to a 0.5 T field?
* 3 N
o 1.5 N
o 6 N
o 12 N
= F = BIL = 0.5 × 3 × 2.
= Every electric motor is this force turning a coil.
> Next: where magnetic fields come from

## p2-bs | Biot–Savart and Ampère | physics | 2 | phys2 | p2-bf
story 4 | Ørsted saw a compass twitch during a lecture, 1820
- Hans Christian Ørsted noticed a compass needle move when he switched on a current nearby.
- Within weeks, Ampère and Biot and Savart had worked out the laws.
- *Electricity and magnetism were connected.*
> Field around a straight wire

quiz 3 | Magnetic field at distance r from a long straight wire carrying current I?
* μ₀I/(2πr)
o μ₀I/(4πr²)
o μ₀I/r
o μ₀Ir
= Ampère's law: B × 2πr = μ₀I.
= Circles around the wire, by the right-hand grip rule.
> Two parallel wires attract

explain 3 | Parallel currents attract
- Each wire's field pushes on the other's current.
- Same direction: attract. Opposite directions: repel.
- The ampere was defined from this force until 2019.
> Solenoids make uniform fields

quiz 3 | Field inside a long solenoid with n turns per metre carrying I?
* μ₀nI
o μ₀I/(2πr)
o μ₀nI/2
o Zero
= Ampère's law on a rectangle half inside: B L = μ₀ n L I.
= Field outside is nearly zero.
> No magnetic monopoles: field lines always close

myth 3 F | Cutting a bar magnet in half gives a separate north pole and south pole.
= You get two smaller magnets, each with both poles.
= ∇·B = 0: no isolated magnetic charges have ever been found.
> Next: induction

## p2-ind | Electromagnetic induction | physics | 2 | phys2 | p2-bs
quiz 3 | A 100-turn coil's flux changes by 0.02 Wb in 0.1 s. Induced EMF?
* 20 V
o 2 V
o 0.2 V
o 200 V
= ε = N ΔΦ/Δt = 100 × 0.02 / 0.1.
> Lenz's sign is energy conservation

explain 3 | Lenz's law is energy conservation
- If induced currents *helped* the change, a nudge would create a runaway current from nothing.
- So they always oppose it.
- *Push a magnet into a coil and you feel resistance: that's the work becoming electrical energy.*
> Eddy currents brake trains

real 3 | Induction hobs heat the pan, not the hob
- A coil under the glass makes a rapidly changing magnetic field.
- It induces eddy currents in the pan's metal base, which heat it directly.
- *Aluminium and glass pans don't work well: the base needs to be magnetic and resistive enough.*
> Transformers use mutual induction

quiz 3 | A transformer has 100 primary turns and 2,000 secondary turns. 230 V in gives…
* 4,600 V out
o 11.5 V out
o 230 V out
o 2,300 V out
= V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p = 20.
= Current steps down by the same factor: power is (ideally) conserved.
> Next: AC circuits

## p2-ac | AC circuits | physics | 2 | phys2 | p2-ind
explain 3 | RMS: the honest average of AC
- Mains voltage is a sine wave. Its average is zero, but heating depends on V².
- RMS = peak/√2. UK mains is 230 V RMS, about 325 V peak.
- *A 230 V AC heater runs exactly like a 230 V DC one.*
> Capacitors and inductors have frequency-dependent resistance

explain 4 | Reactance
- Capacitor: X_C = 1/(ωC). Blocks low frequencies, passes high ones.
- Inductor: X_L = ωL. Blocks high frequencies, passes low ones.
- *Speaker crossovers send bass to the woofer and treble to the tweeter this way.*
> Phasors turn AC into complex algebra

explain 4 | Impedance is complex resistance
- Z_R = R, Z_L = iωL, Z_C = 1/(iωC).
- Then V = IZ works for AC, with Z combining like resistances.
- *The imaginary part is the 90° phase shift.* Electrical engineers write j instead of i.
> RLC resonance

quiz 4 | A series RLC circuit draws maximum current at what frequency?
* ω = 1/√(LC)
o ω = R/L
o ω = 1/(RC)
o ω = 0
= At resonance X_L = X_C, they cancel, and Z = R is minimal.
> Next: Maxwell's equations in action

## p2-emw | EM waves | physics | 2 | phys2 | p2-max
explain 3 | Light is a transverse EM wave
- E and B oscillate perpendicular to each other and to the direction of travel.
- E/B = c in vacuum.
- Radio, microwave, infrared, visible, UV, X-ray, gamma: same thing, different wavelengths.
> Hertz proved the waves exist in 1887

story 4 | Hertz said his discovery had no use
- 1887: Heinrich Hertz generated and detected radio waves with spark gaps.
- Asked about applications, he reportedly said it was of no use whatsoever.
- *By 1899, Marconi was sending wireless signals across the Channel.*
> Visible light is a tiny sliver of the spectrum

quiz 2 | Roughly what wavelength range can human eyes see?
* About 380–750 nm
o About 1–10 μm
o About 10–100 nm
o About 1 mm–1 m
= Violet ~380 nm to red ~750 nm.
= Less than one octave, out of a spectrum spanning over 20 orders of magnitude.
> Light carries momentum, so it can push

real 4 | Solar sails really work
- Light has momentum p = E/c. Reflecting it pushes the sail.
- Japan's IKAROS probe (2010) sailed using sunlight alone.
- *The push is tiny, but it never stops.*
> Next: reflection and refraction from Maxwell

## p2-opt | Optics from electromagnetism | physics | 2 | phys2 | p2-emw
explain 2 | Refraction: light slows down in materials
- Speed in a medium: v = c/n. Glass n ≈ 1.5, water n ≈ 1.33.
- Snell's law: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂.
- *Light bends toward the normal entering a denser medium.*
> Total internal reflection traps light

quiz 3 | Critical angle for light leaving glass (n = 1.5) into air?
* About 42°
o About 30°
o About 60°
o About 90°
= sin θ_c = 1/1.5 ≈ 0.667.
= Beyond 42° the light can't escape: it reflects completely.
> The internet runs on total internal reflection

real 4 | Fibre optics are trapped light
- A glass core with a slightly lower-index cladding keeps light bouncing inside.
- Modern fibre loses only about 0.2 dB per km: a signal travels around 15 km before halving.
- *Undersea cables carry the vast majority of intercontinental internet traffic.*
> Polarisation is the direction E oscillates

explain 3 | Polarised sunglasses
- Glare from water and roads is mostly horizontally polarised (Brewster's angle effect).
- Polarising lenses block horizontal E-field oscillations.
- Tilt your head with polarised glasses and phone screens can go dark: they're polarised too.
> Next: solving for fields exactly

## p2-lap | Laplace's equation and images | physics | 3 | phys2 | p2-opt
explain 4 | In charge-free regions, ∇²V = 0
- Laplace's equation: the potential is the average of its surroundings.
- So V has no local maxima or minima in empty space.
- *Earnshaw's theorem: you can't trap a charge with static electric fields alone.*
> That's why ion traps need oscillating fields

story 4 | Earnshaw's theorem and the Paul trap
- Static fields can't hold a charge stably: there's always an escape direction.
- Wolfgang Paul used a rapidly oscillating field that averages to a trap. Nobel Prize 1989.
- *Ion traps now hold single atoms for quantum computers.*
> Image charges solve conductor problems by trickery

explain 4 | Method of images
- A charge q near a grounded conducting plane.
- Replace the plane with a fake charge −q at the mirror position. The potential on the plane is zero, as required.
- *Uniqueness theorem: if it satisfies the boundary conditions, it's the answer.*
? Why is the method of images valid? :: The uniqueness theorem: a solution matching the boundary conditions is the only solution
> Separation of variables handles boxes and spheres

explain 5 | Separation of variables
- Guess V(x, y) = X(x)Y(y). Laplace's equation splits into two ODEs.
- Boundary conditions pick out sines, sinhs, or Legendre polynomials on spheres.
- *Sum the pieces with Fourier coefficients to match any boundary.*
> Next: multipoles

## p2-mp | Multipole expansion | physics | 3 | phys2 | p2-lap
explain 4 | Multipole expansion: far away, any charge looks simple
- V = monopole (1/r) + dipole (1/r²) + quadrupole (1/r³) + …
- Net charge sets the monopole. Separation of charge sets the dipole.
- *Far enough away, only the first non-zero term matters.*
> Water's dipole explains half of chemistry

real 4 | Water is a strong electric dipole
- The O–H bonds pull electrons toward oxygen, and the bent shape doesn't cancel the dipoles.
- That makes water dissolve salts, form hydrogen bonds, and absorb microwaves.
- *Microwave ovens jiggle water molecules' dipoles back and forth.*
> Next: fields inside materials

## p2-mat | Fields in matter | physics | 3 | phys2 | p2-mp
explain 4 | D and H: fields that track free charges and currents
- Materials polarise (P) and magnetise (M).
- D = ε₀E + P and H = B/μ₀ − M separate free sources from bound ones.
- ∇·D = ρ_free, ∇×H = J_free + ∂D/∂t.
> Ferromagnets remember

explain 4 | Ferromagnetism and hysteresis
- Iron's atomic magnetic moments align in domains.
- Apply a field and domains grow; remove it and some alignment stays.
- *That memory stored data on hard drives for decades.*
> Diamagnetism can levitate a frog

story 5 | The levitating frog, 2000 Ig Nobel Prize
- Water (and so frogs) is weakly diamagnetic: repelled by magnetic fields.
- In a 16 T magnet, Andre Geim levitated a live frog.
- *Ten years later he won a real Nobel Prize, for graphene.*
> Next: radiation from moving charges

## p2-rad | Radiation | physics | 4 | phys2 | p2-mat
explain 5 | Only accelerating charges radiate
- A charge at constant velocity just carries its field along.
- Accelerate it and a kink in the field races outward at c: radiation.
- Larmor: power P = q²a²/(6πε₀c³).
> This doomed the classical atom

story 4 | The classical atom should collapse in about 10⁻¹¹ s
- An electron orbiting a nucleus is accelerating, so it should radiate and spiral in.
- Classical physics predicts collapse in tens of picoseconds.
- *Atoms exist. Quantum mechanics was needed to explain why.*
> Synchrotron light is radiation used on purpose

real 4 | Synchrotrons: X-ray factories
- Electrons forced round a ring at nearly c radiate intense, narrow beams of X-rays.
- Biologists use them to work out protein structures.
- Diamond Light Source in Oxfordshire is one: a ring about half a kilometre round.
> Next: electromagnetism and relativity

## p2-rel | Relativistic electrodynamics | physics | 4 | phys2 | p2-adv
explain 5 | Maxwell's equations were already relativistic
- Lorentz found the transformations that leave them unchanged before Einstein explained why.
- Einstein's 1905 paper was titled *On the Electrodynamics of Moving Bodies*.
- *Special relativity came out of electromagnetism.*
> E and B mix under boosts

quiz 5 | Which quantity is the same for every inertial observer?
* E² − c²B²
o E alone
o B alone
o E + B
= E and B change with the frame, but E² − c²B² and E·B are Lorentz invariants.
= A pure E field in one frame can't become a pure B field in another.
> The four-potential unifies φ and A

explain 5 | Four-vectors package it neatly
- A^μ = (φ/c, A) and J^μ = (cρ, J).
- Lorenz gauge: ∂_μA^μ = 0. Then each component of A^μ obeys a wave equation sourced by J^μ.
- *Charge conservation is ∂_μJ^μ = 0.*
> Physics 2 done. Physics 3: waves, heat and the quantum
